WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:00.870
(applause)

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.180
- So your new Netflix
movie is called Horse Girl,

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.890
so is it about a horse and a girl,

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:06.770
or why is it called that?

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:10.190
- It is about a girl
who used to ride horses,

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.590
and she no longer really does.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.740
But she continues to have

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.000
a current obsession with her horse.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.120
I wouldn't say that's the main...

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.890
That's sort of information
about her character.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.640
It's really about a
socially isolated woman,

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.490
played by me, who begins
to have a series of

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:31.040
surreal dreams and starts
to lose her grip on reality.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.040
- Yeah, I'd say so from that clip.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.720
- Yeah!
(both laughing)

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.060
- The best part is Molly
Shannon's character

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.370
going, "Mmm, No.".

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.727
- I don't believe in that.
- I do not.

00:00:38.727 --> 00:00:41.484
But do you believe in UFO's
or aliens and all that.

00:00:41.484 --> 00:00:42.317
- Absolutely!

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.620
- Okay, I believe that
anything could exist.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.453
I don't know.

00:00:44.453 --> 00:00:47.280
- Well the universe does seem so big.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.520
- Vast.
- Like unimaginably vast.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.680
So also as we were writing this movie,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.920
because I co-wrote it
with my friend Jeff Baena.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.753
- [Kelly] So cool.

00:00:54.753 --> 00:00:55.586
- Thank you.

00:00:55.586 --> 00:00:56.910
First time.
- Go ahead.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.332
Take all the jobs.
- Thanks everyone.

00:00:58.332 --> 00:00:59.723
(laughter)
(applause)

00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:01.812
For the support.

00:01:01.812 --> 00:01:03.589
- I felt it here.

00:01:03.589 --> 00:01:04.737
- I can tell you felt it.

00:01:04.737 --> 00:01:06.327
- I love you!

00:01:06.327 --> 00:01:08.820
- But as we were doing
research we read a lot of

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.100
message boards about
people being abducted,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.790
or people who feel that
they've been abducted,

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.610
and actually there's
a lot of through lines

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.810
to those stories that
we wrote into the movie.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.170
- Wow.
- Also my whole life,

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.300
I mean when I was in middle school,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.330
my mom had this boyfriend who
was really into alien stuff.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.470
We watched a lot of "X-Files"
and stuff like that.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.070
- Yeah, Scully, Mulder, yes.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.520
- Of course.
- I know them all.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.350
- And he definitely told a story to me

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.650
that stuck with me for so
long about his encounter

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.250
with aliens where he was driving,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.930
maybe through New Mexico,
and it was just desert,

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.580
and there were lights behind
him, he thought it was

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.610
a big truck, but then it
came up, went over his car.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.380
Then he pulled over at his
next stop, it was a diner,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.210
and he was eating a
burger and the burger chef

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.510
had the longest fingers he'd ever seen.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.718
- That's not real, he made
that up to scare you, right?

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.010
- As I'm saying it,

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.310
it does sound like he probably
made it up to scare me,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:06.440
but my whole life I've just
thought it was real, so.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:07.950
- I would say I'm not into that at all.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.783
- No
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:08.783 --> 00:02:09.616
- [Alison] I like regular size.

00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:10.603
But if you like it, way to go.

00:02:10.603 --> 00:02:12.370
(clapping)
- Yeah!

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.450
- So you are so great in
"Glow", and now I hear

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.560
there's another season, right?

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.360
- Yes, we're doing our
fourth and final season.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.364
- That's exciting, have
you had such a great time?

00:02:20.364 --> 00:02:21.197
(cheering and applauding)
- [Alison] Thank you.

00:02:21.197 --> 00:02:24.230
- Because I hear that you've
just had the best time

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.710
with all the female... it's a female cast

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.870
and you'd think that
could get a little dicey.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.703
- Oh my God.

00:02:28.703 --> 00:02:29.536
- But it was so good.

00:02:29.536 --> 00:02:30.940
- With a cast of 15 women it is

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.590
the least dramatic thing
I've ever worked on.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:34.423
- [Kelly] That's awesome.

00:02:34.423 --> 00:02:36.820
- And it's the greatest job of my life.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.010
I love it, we get to do everything on it.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.950
You know, we get to do dramatic scenes,

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.270
we get to do really broad comedy,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.880
we get to do stuff that's
really physical in the ring.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.830
Learning how to wrestle changed the way

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.260
that I relate to my body,
it's been really empowering,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.880
and it's just fun, it's just really fun.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.560
I love everyone, Betty Gilpin
has become my best friend.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.630
I feel like sometimes
I think the only point

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.850
of the show was to bring her
as a soulmate into my life.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.790
- I think that that does
happen, I think that we do go

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.400
through different paths and
it's just to meet these people,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.020
and then you go onto something,
I believe that's true.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.000
That's so cool though,
'cause I think you should

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:10.980
love where you work.
- Oh my God.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.042
- You should love all the
people you're working with.

00:03:12.042 --> 00:03:12.875
- It is the best.
(applauding)

00:03:12.875 --> 00:03:15.230
- It's like the life lesson
I learned as I get older.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.480
This is my favorite thing
I've found out about you,

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.100
is you know how to make balloon animals.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:21.933
- Oh, yes!

00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:23.845
- So, why?
(laughing)

00:03:23.845 --> 00:03:26.510
- Well because I worked
for a summer as a clown

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.340
for children's birthday parties...

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.954
- I love this story!
- [Alison] You know.

00:03:30.954 --> 00:03:31.790
As you do.
- So how did that come about,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.383
you were like, "I'm just
gonna start being a clown."?

00:03:33.383 --> 00:03:35.540
- Well it was right after high school,

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.430
or maybe in between my
junior and senior year

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.130
and I had a couple friends
from high school drama,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.267
who were like, "oh, we got
this great job for the summer

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:43.577
"being clowns, you should do it.

00:03:43.577 --> 00:03:45.110
"It's super easy and fun.",

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.550
and the offices were actually in Compton,

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.270
we had a big warehouse where we learned

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.950
how to make balloon animals, paint faces,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.800
and do a few party games with the kids,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.020
and we came with a
duffel bag and a boombox.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.690
So I would drive to this
building in Compton,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.310
do my clown makeup,

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.470
I wore a big yellow afro.
- [Kelly] I love this.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.075
My clown name was Sunny.
(laughing)

00:04:07.075 --> 00:04:10.520
And then I would drive all over
L.A. to all different types.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:11.870
Parties in tiny apartments,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.870
parties in big beautiful mansions,

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.530
and I would just come in by myself,

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.460
put some music on for an hour or two,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.060
paint their faces, make balloon animals.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.640
- That would make me so nervous,

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.143
'cause kids are not the
nicest all the time.

00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:25.920
- Terrifying, terrifying.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.620
To this day I think it might...

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.370
- They can be bullies!
- have been the best training

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.560
for my career as an actor.
- You're right.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.357
- To be fearless, and to really
entertain a crowd of people.

00:04:35.357 --> 00:04:37.040
- And to know that no
matter how hard you try,

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:38.634
you're still gonna suck to someone.

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:39.467
(audience laughing)

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:42.445
- I mean, you haven't seen
my clown work yet Kelly.

00:04:42.445 --> 00:04:45.570
(laughing)
- Pretty outstanding.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.930
I think this would be very
impressive for my kids,

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.950
can you teach me how to
make an animal balloon?

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.570
I wanna do that.
- I definitely can.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.139
- Okay, okay, all right.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:55.299
(laughing)
(audience applauding)

00:04:55.299 --> 00:04:57.260
So wait, okay you want teal?

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:58.718
- Yes I do want teal.
- I'm gonna go red,

00:04:58.718 --> 00:04:59.551
'cause I'm daring.

00:04:59.551 --> 00:05:01.510
- Okay, so the important
thing is that first of all,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.850
when you blow it up you always
wanna leave a little room,

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:04.930
because you gotta fill out the room.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.129
We're gonna start at the
other end of the balloon.

00:05:06.129 --> 00:05:07.720
- Oh 'cause when you blow
it up the air gets pushed.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.360
- Exactly
- Nice, displacement, okay.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.380
- We're gonna make just a
standard, four-legged animal,

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.520
which is a good base zone,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.370
'cause then you can
always elongate the neck,

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:16.360
it's a giraffe.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.870
Shorten everything, it's a poodle.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:18.703
- Yes!

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.090
- We're gonna twist the
balloon into three circles.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:23.400
So you're gonna do one, just twist it.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.233
- I'm just afraid...
- Don't be scared.

00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:26.400
- It's gonna pop, it's totally fine.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.811
And then you, oh!

00:05:27.811 --> 00:05:29.330
- And you're gonna hold onto it,

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:30.310
and then you're gonna do a second circle.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.910
- How much do I have to
twist it for it to stay?

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.920
- Just a few times.
(audience laughing)

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.483
And then you're gonna twist
the third time around.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:40.793
Kelly!

00:05:40.793 --> 00:05:41.626
- I can't even get...

00:05:41.626 --> 00:05:43.500
- Twist and hold, you
have to twist and hold.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:44.710
Don't be afraid of the balloon.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:45.720
- No, I'm not, I'm holding.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.010
- Now hold.
- Hold.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.460
- Now twist this section.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.648
- Oh, oh you do, okay.

00:05:50.648 --> 00:05:52.090
- Good, good.
- Okay.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.820
- I've never taught this
before, it's my clown college.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.190
- Okay.
And now fold it here

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.900
and twist that into that.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:05:59.733
- That into that.
- It seems scary.

00:05:59.733 --> 00:06:01.160
- And these don't pop?

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.950
Are you serious?
- You can still do it!

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:03.783
You can still do it!
- I'm about to have

00:06:03.783 --> 00:06:04.616
a fight with you!

00:06:04.616 --> 00:06:05.449
- You can still do it,
you can still do it,

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:06.510
you can still do it!

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.990
- Okay wait, so then you take that...

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.920
- Into that, go, go, go, go.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.160
You can do it, it's not gonna pop.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.520
It's not gonna pop.
(balloon squeaking)

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.419
Don't fear the balloon, great!

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:17.400
Great, okay great!

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.065
So now you have to...

00:06:19.065 --> 00:06:22.350
- Yes!
(audience cheering)

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:23.183
I did it!

00:06:23.183 --> 00:06:25.191
- I feel like it's a head on a stick.

00:06:25.191 --> 00:06:26.660
- Looks like this, what do you do next?

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.710
- So then you do that a few more times.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.957
So you make a little neck,
and then you do... right.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:35.072
So you twist,
- [Both] then twist again.

00:06:35.072 --> 00:06:35.905
- [Alison] Then you're gonna fold.

00:06:35.905 --> 00:06:36.738
- [Kelly] You do it again.
- [Alison] Again.

00:06:36.738 --> 00:06:37.910
- [Kelly] You do it again.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.515
- I'm gonna move forward
but I'm gonna double back.

00:06:39.515 --> 00:06:40.348
- You go ahead.
- I'm gonna double back...

00:06:40.348 --> 00:06:42.280
- We're gonna be here for
the length of a movie.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.545
- And help so then you
do that a few more times.

00:06:45.545 --> 00:06:46.666
- [Kelly] Like this?
- [Alison] Yes, yes, yes.

00:06:46.666 --> 00:06:48.090
- Just do that? Okay.
- Do it, do it, do it.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.313
I just like to encourage more than teach.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.389
And then you do it again.

00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:55.521
- Oh, do it again.

00:06:55.521 --> 00:06:57.944
- And next thing you know,
you have a little dog.

00:06:57.944 --> 00:06:59.512
- Wait, wait, so you do it again?

00:06:59.512 --> 00:07:00.720
- Do it again.
- Do it again.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.620
- And then one more time, no wait,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:03.800
you have to do another leg.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.173
- Another leg, we're gonna edit this.

00:07:06.173 --> 00:07:07.500
- This is great.
- It's gonna be great.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.550
- You're gonna do it one more time.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.910
- It's gonna be great.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.913
Holy word I can't say on daytime.

00:07:12.913 --> 00:07:13.746
(audience laughing)

00:07:13.746 --> 00:07:15.113
- Here, wait, you know what you can do?

00:07:15.113 --> 00:07:16.500
